{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___ . I’m Dr. ___ . You were brought in because your feeding tube wasn’t working and you had trouble breathing during transport. I’ll explain everything, and you can nod or shake your head if you understand or have questions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. In the ER, your oxygen dropped, but respiratory therapy suctioned your trach and you improved quickly. We also noticed your trach tube was leaking air, so it needs to be replaced."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods, looks concerned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also checked your urine because your white blood cell count was high. It showed signs of infection — lots of bacteria and white cells. So we’re treating you for a urinary tract infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Raises eyebrows."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s not unusual for you given your history. Because you’ve had serious infections before with resistant bacteria, we’re using strong antibiotics — cefepime and daptomycin — through an IV."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also tried to clear your feeding tube with diet coke in the ER, but it didn’t work. Surgery was consulted, and they recommend replacing it. We’re waiting on a part for your trach exchange too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Slight frown."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I know it’s frustrating. But you’re stable, and we’ll get both tubes fixed. For now, you’re on a trach mask and your breathing is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2136-06-10 22:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods, relaxes slightly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs are improving — your white count is coming down. The urine culture showed mixed bacteria, likely from contamination, so we’re sticking with broad coverage due to your history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also checked your chest X-ray. There’s some opacity in the left lung base — could be atelectasis or mild aspiration. But you’re not feverish or coughing, so we don’t think it’s pneumonia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Shakes head — denies cough."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. So we’ll keep monitoring. Your warfarin was held because your INR was high. We’ll restart it once it’s safer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2136-06-11 08:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2136-06-11 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Quick update — the trach exchange part came in. The respiratory therapist replaced it this morning. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2136-06-11 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods — feels okay."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2136-06-11 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Your breathing sounds clearer. We’ll repeat the chest X-ray to confirm the trach position."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2136-06-11 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news — your GJ tube was evaluated today. It wasn’t completely blocked. The radiologist flushed it with saline and contrast, and it worked fine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Looks surprised."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes! So we didn’t need to replace it. We restarted your tube feeds. But later, it clogged again briefly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Frowns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "But — we cleared it with diet coke using a 5 cc syringe. That’s important: you must use a 5 cc syringe to generate enough pressure. Smaller ones won’t work."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2136-06-12 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods firmly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2136-06-12 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your leg pain has been worse, hasn’t it? You’ve been grimacing when we move your legs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2136-06-12 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods — yes, pain in calves and thighs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2136-06-12 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We think it’s neuropathic — nerve pain from your diabetes. So we’re doubling your gabapentin, increasing your fentanyl patch to 200 mcg, and adding a capsaicin cream for your legs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2136-06-12 15:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods — looks relieved."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final update — your white count is down to 11.2, which is much better. Your infection is responding. The chest X-ray shows some atelectasis, but no pneumonia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go back to extended care today. But you need to finish the antibiotics — cefepime and daptomycin — for two more weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Looks concerned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Don’t worry — we placed a PICC line yesterday so you can get IV antibiotics there. And we’ll check your CK weekly — that’s a muscle enzyme — because of daptomycin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Remember: use a 5 cc syringe with diet coke daily to prevent clogs. And your warfarin will restart soon — INR check first. Any questions?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Shakes head — no questions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2136-06-13 07:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You did well. We’ll make sure your care team knows all the changes. Get some rest before discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2136-06-13 12:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The PICC line placement went smoothly. The X-ray confirmed it’s in the right spot in the vein. You’re all set for antibiotics at the facility."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2136-06-13 12:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just confirming — your trach is secure, GJ tube is working, infection is improving, and pain is better managed. Discharge paperwork is ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods — smiles slightly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll continue all your usual meds. The new ones are the higher fentanyl patch, more gabapentin, capsaicin cream, and the two IV antibiotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "And — very important — use only a 5 cc syringe to flush the tube. That’s what makes the difference."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods firmly — understands."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Alright. Transport will arrive soon. Take care, and we’ll follow up with your team."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2136-06-13 15:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Nods — waves slightly with hand."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2136-06-10 22:00:00",
    "admission_end": "2136-06-13 18:42:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 68-year-old male with a history of stroke, tracheostomy, GJ tube, atrial fibrillation on anticoagulation, diabetes, and recurrent infections was admitted for a clogged GJ tube, urinary tract infection, and tracheostomy leak. He developed hypoxia during transport due to a mucus plug, which resolved with suctioning. He was treated with broad-spectrum antibiotics—cefepime and daptomycin—due to a history of resistant organisms, despite contaminated urine culture. The tracheostomy was successfully exchanged, and the GJ tube was unclogged using a 5 cc syringe and diet coke. A PICC line was placed for outpatient antibiotic completion. His infection resolved, leg pain was managed with increased gabapentin, fentanyl patch, and capsaicin cream, and he was discharged to extended care with follow-up plans for INR monitoring and CK checks.",
    "problems": [
      "Mechanical complication of gastrostomy",
      "Urinary tract infection, site not specified",
      "Other tracheostomy complications",
      "Polyneuropathy in diabetes",
      "Atrial fibrillation",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Depressive disorder, not elsewhere classified",
      "Peripheral vascular disease, unspecified",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Paraplegia",
      "Late effects of cerebrovascular disease, other paralytic syndrome, bilateral",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Long-term (current) use of insulin",
      "Bed confinement status"
    ]
  }
}
